(*
UNSAFE MUTATION OF ORIGINAL JOBHIRING-INSPIRED WORKFLOW
  Mutation: notify omits the all-other-published-rows-classified completion scan.
  All init, insertion, recursion and safety properties are unchanged.
  The faulty notification genuinely returns at a committed Finished boundary.
  With two inserted rows, select one then notify immediately leaves the other
  Pending, violating notified-record classification; this is not an internal
  transient violation or a null-only artificial mutation.

Sources/relationship:
  RAS: Verification of Data-Aware Processes via Array-Based Systems,
  https://arxiv.org/pdf/1806.11459, Example 4.2 and Appendix G: protected
  application insertion; notification implies a winner/loser outcome.
  RAB: https://github.com/AlessandroGianola/RAB-verification,
  RAB-benchmark/E+P17.txt: ReceiveApplication/RegisterApplication/Closing;
  DetermineWinner is bulk; its null-winning-applicant property is distinct.
  Read design notes: experiments/transaction-benchmark-ideas.md, JobHiring;
  /Users/kes/.hermes/profiles/tetra/cache/scratch/txbench-mcmt.md, section 2.
  Syntax checked against examples/german_looped.cub, german_pfs.cub and
  tests/state-dispatch-scheduler/models/bindings-unsafe.cub.
  This is newly written finite-status Cubicle code, NOT a copied MCMT input.
  No RAS/RAB theorem, full database semantics, or foreign-key proof carries over.

Declared simplifications and shared parameters:
  One hiring workflow/vacancy; arbitrary applicant slots indexed by proc.
  No fixed applicant count. Each slot is inserted at most once; intake may
  insert multiple slots before selection. proc is the symbolic identity domain;
  Applicant[i] := i identifies an applicant with its slot (injective identities,
  not an independent UserId/database sort). Identified=False is the explicit
  null/uninitialized abstraction: no distinguished proc null constant is used.
  Eligibility is nondeterministic finite data; no CV, arithmetic score, external
  database, separate employer, message delivery/retries, crash or rollback.
  Selection may choose any eligible registered applicant. No eligible applicant
  may mean no completion: SAFE does not imply liveness, fairness or deadlock freedom.

State and init:
  Workflow finite task statuses: Intake, Receiving, Registering, Classifying,
  Finished. Each Row is Vacant, Received, Registered. Published is visibility;
  Identified validates Applicant; Qualification is Unknown/Eligible/Ineligible;
  Result is Pending/Won/Lost. Winner is meaningful iff HasWinner=True.
  Initially Intake, no winner, no notification; every row vacant/unpublished,
  unidentified, unknown qualification and pending result. Applicant[i]=i supplies
  harmless default data; no identity is valid before Identified becomes True.
  Winner is unconstrained initially, because HasWinner=False. Init is consistent.

Transaction-level workflow contract (specification, not inferred isolation):
  receive(i) -> register_eligible(i) OR register_ineligible(i) -> commit(i)
  is a serialized database service operation. The SAME i is bound throughout.
  No other workflow operation can run until return to the neutral boundary;
  task guards also explicitly forbid another insertion/selection mid-insert.
  Published is deliberately set early: transient rows are incompletely
  initialized, so published consistency is a COMMITTED-state requirement.
  This stronger whole-workflow serialization is a declared target contract;
  RAS insertion protection alone does NOT justify excluding arbitrary actions.
  select(w) -> classify_loser(i)* -> notify(w) is a second exclusive operation:
  intake closes, winner w stays fixed, each pending nonwinner becomes Lost.
  The recursive walk is genuine: select does NOT bulk-initialize loser outcomes.
  notify's completion scan requires every other published row Lost. This guard
  is the operational completion condition, not an assumed safety invariant.
  For finite populations the walk can finish; each loop consumes one pending
  published loser. A completed walk has the same outcomes as the declared bulk
  assignment (winner Won, other published rows Lost). No source equivalence
  theorem has been proved, and there is no claim about infinite executions.

Boundary safety properties below (no supplied invariant assumptions):
  Published implies Registered, Identified and known Qualification.
  Every Won row is published/identified/eligible and matches the valid Winner.
  HasWinner refers to a published, identified, eligible registered winner row.
  At most one row Won; after Notified every published row is Won or Lost.
  Notification also requires a winner and Finished workflow status.
  Notification is nonvacuous: insert two eligible slots a,b; select(a);
  classify_loser(b); notify(a). Both received rows remain published, a wins,
  b loses. With one eligible row, select then notify can complete immediately.

Tests, exact commands and observed outcomes:
  python3 /Users/kes/.hermes/profiles/tetra/cache/scratch/txmodels-jobhiring/run_checks.py
  /Users/kes/cubicle/cubicle.opt -j 0 -nocolor -tx bwd -nodes 5000 /Users/kes/.hermes/profiles/tetra/cache/scratch/txmodels-jobhiring/jobhiring-unsafe-notify.cub
  /Users/kes/cubicle/cubicle.opt -j 0 -nocolor -tx all -nodes 5000 /Users/kes/.hermes/profiles/tetra/cache/scratch/txmodels-jobhiring/jobhiring-unsafe-notify.cub
  Both modes UNSAFE/exit 1: 105 visited nodes, 1066 solver calls, support 2.
  Counterexample: Init -> receive(2) -> register_eligible(2) -> commit(2) ->
  receive(1) -> register_eligible(1) -> commit(1) -> select(2) -> notify(2).
  Finished/Notified=True is committed; row 2 Won; row 1 still Pending (unsafe[16]).
  Safe sibling is SAFE/0 in both modes (92 nodes, 849 solver calls).
  Safe-relation reachability probe is expected UNSAFE/1 in both modes: 3 inserted
  rows, select(1), classify_loser(3), classify_loser(2), notify(1), proving a
  completed nonvacuous notification and two real recursive steps (111 nodes,
  2574 solver calls). All exact sibling commands appear in jobhiring-safe.cub.
  Runner uses cwd=this directory, external 30-second Popen.wait deadline and
  process-group SIGKILL; -nodes 5000. No timeouts. Full raw logs and runs.json
  retain commands, elapsed times, hashes, counters and expected/actual outcomes.
  The contract description above states the SAFE sibling's intended behavior;
  this mutant violates its completion condition, not its serialization contract.
  No performance conclusion: functional checks can overlap other workers.
*)

type workflow = Intake | Receiving | Registering | Classifying | Finished
type row_state = Vacant | Received | Registered
type qualification = Unknown | Eligible | Ineligible
type outcome = Pending | Won | Lost

var Workflow : workflow
var Winner : proc
var HasWinner : bool
var Notified : bool
array Row[proc] : row_state
array Published[proc] : bool
array Identified[proc] : bool
array Applicant[proc] : proc
array Qualification[proc] : qualification
array Result[proc] : outcome

init (i) {
  Workflow = Intake && HasWinner = False && Notified = False &&
  Row[i] = Vacant && Published[i] = False && Identified[i] = False &&
  Applicant[i] = i && Qualification[i] = Unknown && Result[i] = Pending
}

unsafe (i) { Published[i] = True && Row[i] <> Registered }
unsafe (i) { Published[i] = True && Identified[i] = False }
unsafe (i) { Published[i] = True && Qualification[i] = Unknown }
unsafe (i) { Published[i] = True && Applicant[i] <> i }
unsafe (i) { Result[i] = Won && Published[i] = False }
unsafe (i) { Result[i] = Won && Identified[i] = False }
unsafe (i) { Result[i] = Won && Qualification[i] <> Eligible }
unsafe (i) { Result[i] = Won && HasWinner = False }
unsafe (i) { Result[i] = Won && Winner <> i }
unsafe (i) { HasWinner = True && Winner = i && Published[i] = False }
unsafe (i) { HasWinner = True && Winner = i && Row[i] <> Registered }
unsafe (i) { HasWinner = True && Winner = i && Identified[i] = False }
unsafe (i) { HasWinner = True && Winner = i && Qualification[i] <> Eligible }
unsafe (i) { HasWinner = True && Winner = i && Result[i] <> Won }
unsafe (i j) { Result[i] = Won && Result[j] = Won }
unsafe (i) { Notified = True && Published[i] = True && Result[i] = Pending }
unsafe { Notified = True && HasWinner = False }
unsafe { Notified = True && Workflow <> Finished }

(* Receive reserves and begins constructing a visible row; internal only. *)
transition receive(i)
requires { Workflow = Intake && Row[i] = Vacant } {
  Workflow := Receiving;
  Row[i] := Received;
  Published[i] := True
}
triggers register_eligible(i) or register_ineligible(i)

(* Abstract external eligibility data is chosen here; identity is initialized. *)
triggered transition register_eligible(i)
requires { Workflow = Receiving && Row[i] = Received } {
  Applicant[i] := i;
  Identified[i] := True;
  Qualification[i] := Eligible;
  Workflow := Registering
}
triggers commit(i)

triggered transition register_ineligible(i)
requires { Workflow = Receiving && Row[i] = Received } {
  Applicant[i] := i;
  Identified[i] := True;
  Qualification[i] := Ineligible;
  Workflow := Registering
}
triggers commit(i)

(* Completed insertion restores the boundary; intake remains repeatable. *)
triggered transition commit(i)
requires { Workflow = Registering && Row[i] = Received && Identified[i] = True } {
  Row[i] := Registered;
  Workflow := Intake
}

(* Close intake and select a valid winner. Other rows stay Pending for the loop. *)
transition select(w)
requires { Workflow = Intake && Published[w] = True && Row[w] = Registered &&
           Identified[w] = True && Qualification[w] = Eligible } {
  Workflow := Classifying;
  HasWinner := True;
  Winner := w;
  Result[w] := Won
}
triggers classify_loser(_) or notify(w)

(* Arbitrary changing participant; no bulk update disguised as a recursive loop. *)
triggered transition classify_loser(i)
requires { Workflow = Classifying && Published[i] = True &&
           Result[i] = Pending && i <> Winner } {
  Result[i] := Lost
}
triggers classify_loser(_) or notify(_)

(* BUG: completed notification can skip pending losers; no completion scan. *)
triggered transition notify(w)
requires { Workflow = Classifying && HasWinner = True && Winner = w &&
           Published[w] = True && Result[w] = Won } {
  Notified := True;
  Workflow := Finished
}
